SaturatingSourcePair.tex_statement:
\begin{definition}
For a blow-up instance and two source clusters $X_i,X_j$, the bipartite source graph $H[X_i,X_j]$ is a \emph{saturating source pair} if it is a matching saturating the smaller side: either there is an injection $\tau:X_i\to X_j$ such that $xy\in E(H)$ exactly when $y=\tau(x)$, or there is an injection $\tau:X_j\to X_i$ such that $xy\in E(H)$ exactly when $x=\tau(y)$.  Thus the definition records both the matching property and that every vertex on at least one side is covered.
\end{definition}

SourceClusterPadding.tex_statement:
\begin{lemma}[Padding source clusters to host capacity]
Let $(H,G,R,(X_i),(V_i),(A^i))$ be a blow-up instance with candidacy graphs, and suppose the candidacy graphs are lower-$(\varepsilon,d)$-super-regular, where $0<\varepsilon\leq\eta^2$, $\eta\leq1$, and $d\leq1$.  On the vertex set \noderef{PaddedSource}, there is a graph $H^+$, a blow-up instance with the same $G$ and $R$, and candidacy graphs $(A^{+,i})$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the original vertices induce exactly $H$, every dummy vertex is isolated, original vertices retain their cluster, and a dummy indexed by $i$ belongs to cluster $i$;
\item the host cluster map is unchanged and $|X_i^+|=|V_i|$ for every $i$;
\item $A^{+,i}$ restricts to $A^i$ on $X_i$, while every dummy in $X_i^+\setminus X_i$ is adjacent in $A^{+,i}$ to every vertex of $V_i$;
\item $\Delta(H^+)\leq\Delta(H)$, every lower-super-regularity assertion for the host instance is preserved with the same parameters, and the padded candidacy graphs are lower-$(\eta,d)$-super-regular;
\item restriction along the inclusion $V(H)\hookrightarrow V(H^+)$ sends every compatible embedding of $H^+$ into $G$ to a compatible embedding of $H$ into $G$, and sends every $q$-vertex-spread law on the former embeddings to a $q$-vertex-spread law on the latter embeddings.
\end{enumerate}
All graph, cluster, and candidacy assertions above are literal equalities or if-and-only-if statements.
\end{lemma}

SuperRegularPair.tex_statement:
\begin{definition}
Let $G$ be a simple graph on an arbitrary vertex type, let $V_1,V_2$ be arbitrary finite vertex subsets, and let $\varepsilon,d$ be arbitrary extended nonnegative reals.  The ordered pair $(V_1,V_2)$ is $(\varepsilon,d)$-super-regular if it is $(\varepsilon,d)$-regular in the sense of \noderef{RegularPair}, every $v\in V_1$ has between $(d-\varepsilon)|V_2|$ and $(d+\varepsilon)|V_2|$ neighbors in $V_2$, and every $w\in V_2$ has between $(d-\varepsilon)|V_1|$ and $(d+\varepsilon)|V_1|$ neighbors in $V_1$.  The paper uses this predicate for finite ambient graphs, disjoint cluster fibers, $\varepsilon>0$, and $0<d<1$.
\end{definition}

SuperRegularSubgraph.tex_statement:
\begin{lemma}[Super-regular subgraph]
For every $0<\varepsilon'<1$ and every $0<d<1$, there are $0<\varepsilon<\varepsilon'$ and $n_0\in\mathbb N$ with the following property.  Let $G$ be a finite bipartite graph with bipartition $\{U,W\}$, where $|U|=|W|=n\geq n_0$.  If $(U,W)$ is lower $(\varepsilon,d+\varepsilon)$-regular and every vertex has at least $dn$ neighbors in the opposite class, then $G$ has a spanning subgraph $G'\subseteq G$ for which $(U,W)$ is $(\varepsilon',d^2/2)$-super-regular.
\end{lemma}

